"use strict";(self.webpackChunkultimatelearning=self.webpackChunkultimatelearning||[]).push([[667],{1667:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"topic":{"id":"exceptions","title":"Exception Handling","overviewSimple":"Imagine a toy robot sometimes trips over obstacles. Instead of stopping everything, it has a way to catch the problem and keep working. Java exceptions let programs handle unexpected situations gracefully.","overviewTechnical":["Exception handling in Java allows programs to catch and manage runtime errors without crashing.","Core keywords: try, catch, finally, throw, and throws.","Exceptions are classified as checked (compile-time) and unchecked (runtime).","You can create custom exception classes by extending Exception or RuntimeException.","Proper handling ensures resources are freed and the program continues smoothly."],"story":"Think of a robot following a path. If it encounters a block, it doesn\u2019t stop; it signals an alert and continues. Java exceptions work in a similar way to handle errors without breaking the program.","realWorldAnalogy":"Exceptions are like having safety nets in a circus \u2014 if something goes wrong, performers are protected and can continue performing safely.","keyPoints":["Catch runtime errors","Differentiate checked vs unchecked exceptions","Custom exceptions possible","Use finally for cleanup","Prevents program crashes"],"exampleSimple":"try {\\n    int result = 10 / 0;\\n} catch(ArithmeticException e) {\\n    System.out.println(\\"Cannot divide by zero\\");\\n}","exampleTechnical":["public class ExceptionDemo {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        try {\\n            int[] numbers = {1,2,3};\\n            System.out.println(numbers[5]);\\n        } catch(ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {\\n            System.out.println(\\"Index out of bounds!\\");\\n        } finally {\\n            System.out.println(\\"Execution finished\\");\\n        }\\n    }\\n}","class CustomException extends Exception {\\n    CustomException(String message) { super(message); }\\n}\\n\\npublic class TestCustom {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        try {\\n            throw new CustomException(\\"Something went wrong!\\");\\n        } catch(CustomException e) {\\n            System.out.println(e.getMessage());\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],"extraNotes":["Always handle exceptions at the level where they can be meaningfully resolved.","Unchecked exceptions often indicate programming errors; use them carefully."],"bestPracticesSimple":["Catch only what you can handle","Use specific exceptions instead of generic Exception","Always release resources in finally or use try-with-resources"],"bestPracticesAdvanced":["Create meaningful custom exceptions","Avoid swallowing exceptions silently","Log exception details for debugging"]}}')}}]);